A||||7||Denny||Important: if you haven't seen this thread . . .||||4||
Z||000000||Denny||10-09-2001||05:00 PM||||I think this thread,  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/010812.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/010812.html</A> , is important enough for me to post my reply to it here.<P>+++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++<P>Damn! Sorry to hear that!<BR>I doubt very much that it was the distributor seal. A more likely suspect is the o-ring seal at the fuel-rail fitting for the cold-start tube. That o-ring breaks down with age and leaks fuel under pressure. I've replaced the rings on three V6's I've owned, and we had to do Gargoyle86's a couple of years ago.<P>The lesson here is if you smell gas at the engine compartment, especially just after shutting the engine off, look for a leak at this fitting. You can see it just past the right side of the distributor cap, at the end of the fuel rail. If it's leaking DO NOT DRIVE THE CAR! It's a fire waiting to happen!<P>The O-ring kit part # is 17111954.  <A HREF="http://www.gmpartsdirect.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.gmpartsdirect.com/</A> lists the kit for $7.68 +shipping, and it's an easy job.<P>I wrote an article about it for the MAFOA newsletter, I'll see if I can get Bob (Gargoyle86) to post it on the Pride website. We'll let you know . . .<BR>||||reg||4
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||10-09-2001||05:50 PM||||My thought as well. Oil will not ignite with a stray spark. Gas . . different story.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Denny||10-09-2001||09:15 PM||||Here's the link to the Technical page at   <A HREF="http://www.fieropride.com/technical.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieropride.com/technical.htm</A> .<P>Select "Cold Start O-Ring Replacement" (or any other article you want  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">).<p>[This message has been edited by Denny (edited 10-09-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000003||roadwarrior||10-09-2001||10:26 PM||||what are you talking about? oil won't ignite? of course oil will ignite, ever heard of spontanious combustion? well it happens to oily rags, all it takes is heat and a spark to light up an oil leak.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||10-10-2001||05:26 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by roadwarrior:<BR><B>what are you talking about? oil won't ignite? of course oil will ignite, ever heard of spontanious combustion? well it happens to oily rags, all it takes is heat and a spark to light up an oil leak.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Oil is a lot harder to ignite than gas!<BR>I don't think that I'm wrong on this. The given condition stands to reason that the oil would generate that famous hot oil smell before it burns. An oil leak of that magnitude (if it was oil) would go back to poor maintenance. I wouldn't think that would apply here but I'm not sure of that either. I know that would not be allowed under my hood (dis. 'O' ring leak)<BR>I'm not absolutely sure of the flash point of motor oil is, but I can find out if you like.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Denny||10-10-2001||07:27 AM||||It was not my intent to start an argument here. I suppose I could have said "It's also possible . . ." instead of "I doubt . . ."<P>Anyway, I agree that an oil leak of sufficient volume dumping oil on the headers could also start a fire, especially on the stock headers that haven't been modified to remove the excess metal from the inside. The excess metal blocks the exhaust flow and gets hot enough to cause the headers to glow red at those points. Those and the stock cat are the only parts of the exhaust system that get sufficiently hot enough to ignite oil.<P>So, it <I>is</I> possible. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000006||Indiana_resto_guy||10-10-2001||08:55 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Denny:<BR><B>It was not my intent to start an argument here. I suppose I could have said "It's also possible . . ." instead of "I doubt . . ."<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>No argument here. I just felt the need to clairify my statement. Both are possible, I think that oil would have given a clue before hand is all.<P>||||reg||1
